WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.720
This is a family saga.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.510
An extended family relationship.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.690
We go a long way back,

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:21.500
Seven or eight hundred million years back
when multi-celled organisms first set off

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.730
from our single celled ancestors on the tree
of life.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.860
Something interesting happened.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.140
We never left bacteria behind.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.140
We took them with us.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.300
It was a partnership from day zero.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:40.450
They've been living inside and on us for the
entire ride up the evolutionary tree.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.160
We depend upon them and they depend upon us.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.750
This wonderful cooperation where we both benefit.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.183
Yet for most of our history, we didn't know
they were there.

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:56.530
The first evidence of microbes came in the
late 1600s.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:59.970
A Dutchman by the name of Van Leeuwenhoek.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:04.030
He built the first microscopes and described
the first microbes.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:11.110
An unbelievably great company of living animalcules…
very prettily a-moving through water.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:15.380
He was pretty enthralled with these microorganisms.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.860
But wonder gave way to fear.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.790
As scientists discovered that many of these
tiny creatures had been making us sick.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:28.810
These are bad organisms, truly horrible pathogens
that have plagued human existence

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:33.720
which led us to believe that eliminating them
from our environment, from the water we drink

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:40.211
and the food we eat, and from our bodies would
usher in a new era of public health, and it

00:01:40.211 --> 00:01:41.920
did.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.500
Hygienic practices lead to a spike in human
life span.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:53.369
New Antibiotics like penicillin became wonder
drugs, saving hundreds of millions of lives.

00:01:53.369 --> 00:02:03.980
It seemed the only good bacteria was a dead
bacteria, but our ideas about germs are changing.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:10.729
We are beginning to understand the very subtle
roles they play in regulating our own health.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:15.010
They help us digest our food and fight off
dangerous invaders.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:21.019
We don't know whether a human would be able
to live any meaningful existence in the absence

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:23.019
of microbes.

00:02:23.019 --> 00:02:28.650
Inside every one of us is an entire ecosystem,
a microbiome.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:32.049
And scientists began noticing something else.

00:02:32.049 --> 00:02:38.569
What led to this extension of human life and
reduce human suffering and also may have brought

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:41.239
with it their own set of problems.

00:02:41.239 --> 00:02:43.590
Many diseases are on the table

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.950
obesity, heart disease

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.269
chronic inflammatory diseases

00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:54.939
Things like Crohn's disease, diabetes, many
others, all on the rise.

00:02:54.939 --> 00:03:03.129
Our war on microbes may have caused unintended
collateral damage our own health, so getting

00:03:03.129 --> 00:03:09.840
back on good terms with our long-time fellow
travelers could mean the rebirth of a beautiful

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:10.840
friendship.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.709
By the end of today's program you may have
a very different appreciation for the bugs

00:03:15.709 --> 00:03:20.010
in our environment, particularly bacteria,
which is what we're talking about today.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:24.189
The first thing you need to understand is
that they vastly outnumber us.

00:03:24.189 --> 00:03:30.270
Each one of us has about 30 trillion cells,
human cells that are uniquely ours, but did

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:36.209
you know that living in and on us, are 39
trillion bacteria.

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:37.239
Okay.

00:03:37.239 --> 00:03:40.219
Without further ado, let's introduce our participants.

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:45.090
Our first participant is a professor of medicine,
microbiology, and immunology at Stanford.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.809
He's a pioneer in the study of the human microbiome.

00:03:47.809 --> 00:03:53.700
Please welcome David Relman.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:59.079
Our next participant is director of the Center
of the Microbiome Innovation at the University

00:03:59.079 --> 00:04:00.669
of California San Diego.

00:04:00.669 --> 00:04:04.620
He's the author of Follow Your Gut and Dirt
Is Good.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:05.709
Please welcome.

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:06.980
Rob Knight.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:13.109
Our third participant is director of the Wisconsin
Institute for Discovery at the University

00:04:13.109 --> 00:04:14.849
of Wisconsin Madison.

00:04:14.849 --> 00:04:17.730
She was a science advisor to President Barack
Obama.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.290
Please welcome Jo Handelsman.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:27.840
In rounding out, this distinguished panel
is professor of medicine and microbiology

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.200
at New York University School of Medicine,
and the author of Missing Microbes, will talk

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.580
about that in a bit.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:40.530
Please welcome Martin Blaser.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.900
David, let's start with you.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:48.430
So we evolved with microbes from the very
beginning, but, and we just told everybody,

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:52.381
we all have our own little world living in
and on us, but when do we acquire that, when

00:04:52.381 --> 00:04:54.010
do we get it?

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:58.510
So the beginning is a little bit, a little
bit uncertain.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:03.620
We forget that our own individual histories
begin before birth, which means in this case

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:10.700
that our own fetal development was influenced
by our mother's microbes and her microbiome.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:17.110
We have probably a little bit of direct encounter
with some of those microbes or parts of them

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:22.940
before we're born, but then everything changes
at the time of birth, and it's that passage

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:31.370
through, either the birth canal or out through
the abdominal wall that causes a sudden change

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.840
in our relationship with the microbial world.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.520
And Rob you, you've had some interesting thoughts
about this and a personal experience.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.740
Tell us a little bit about your daughter,
correct?

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.660
And what happened there?

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:45.660
Yeah, sure.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:50.620
So after our daughter was born, as you know,
when you have kids, things don't always go

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:51.700
according to plan.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:56.310
And that was true for us and we wound up having
to have an unplanned c-section for medical

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:57.310
reasons.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:00.650
And straight after she was born, we coated
here with the microbes that she, she, she

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:02.580
would have had had she come out of the regular
way.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:07.180
So you, you feel that strongly that that exposure
in the birth canal is-

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:11.080
we, we, we felt that strongly as parents.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:15.750
Now the AAP recommends that you not do this
now, but this a lot of public interest in

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.700
it until the research has done.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:21.990
So it's not something you should do from a
clinical perspective right now.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:26.530
Of course, as a parent, you're probably going
to have to do a lot of things that are not

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:28.370
100 percent based on evidence.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:29.370
That's right.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.370
That's true.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:33.860
I can tell you from experience, you know,
we saw in the, in the, in the brief piece

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.680
on a whole bunch of diseases that rolled by.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.000
There seems to be some suggestion that there
is an autoimmune component.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.340
By that I mean that the microbiome somehow
interacts with our immune system.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:52.020
Martin, can you talk about that and just to
set the stage here, what, how might these

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.930
interactions be happening?

00:06:54.930 --> 00:07:00.590
So mostly I talk about the microbes in the
gut and that's where a big part of the immune

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:05.700
system is--in the wall of the intestine, and
it's pretty clear that there's interaction

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:10.250
between the microbes and the immune system
and vice versa, and it's becoming more and

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:14.870
more clear that the microbiome is training
the immune system, it's teaching the immune

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:20.210
system what are, what are the good bugs, what
are the bad bugs, and if that training is

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:25.730
off, if it's misaligned, then there's a risk
that the immune system won't develop in the

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:26.730
proper manner.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:31.120
So that's, that's what we think are driving
some of the immunologic diseases.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:32.820
Two-way street there.

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:33.870
Yes.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.990
Before we go any further, we need to sort
of set the stage for how a lot of the work

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.770
in your area has done, and it's done with
mice.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:48.960
Joe, can you explain a little bit about how
you use the mice in this very unique way?

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:54.280
Well, the mice have been particularly powerful
because they can be raised in a sterile environment

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:59.311
so they can can be delivered by Cesarean section
and kept sterile in a bubble, and then they

00:07:59.311 --> 00:08:04.740
can be inoculated with any microbes you want,
and so it's the only system we have where

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:13.260
we can do a very clean mammalian microbiome
or microbiome-free gut, and then add back

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:18.139
one organism, a few organisms or the entire
microbiome from the mouse or even another

00:08:18.139 --> 00:08:20.629
organism like a human.

00:08:20.629 --> 00:08:25.380
And mice are, is this primarily the way most
of the work is done?

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:29.870
Well, one of the powerful things in the microbiome
field is that it brings together many kinds

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:30.870
of data.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:36.289
It's epidemiological data on humans looking
at large populations and correlations, and

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:41.740
then experiments on humans in situations where
we can actually do the experiments with variables

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:47.079
on humans, and then mice and other model systems
play a major role.

00:08:47.079 --> 00:08:49.970
Briefly back to humans though.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:54.529
When we're first born, we acquire our, the
beginnings of our microbiome.

00:08:54.529 --> 00:08:55.559
When does that stabilize?

00:08:55.559 --> 00:09:01.920
When do we say you've got it, this is you
and how long does that take?

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:02.920
Rob.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:06.689
For the gut microbiome, it's about two to
three years, and then for other sites, like

00:09:06.689 --> 00:09:07.689
with the skin on the mouth.

00:09:07.689 --> 00:09:08.779
That's a lot faster.

00:09:08.779 --> 00:09:10.910
So within a few months.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:11.910
And yeah, go ahead.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:18.529
I would just add, I agree, the vast majority
of what we see easily is established by about

00:09:18.529 --> 00:09:19.690
two or three years.

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:23.829
I think we all would agree though, that there
have to be some other in really important

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:29.120
but more subtle features that take a little
bit longer, well into later childhood, maybe

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:31.420
even at the time of adolescence and beyond.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:32.870
and even even through old age.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.750
So the first three years is where you have
the most dramatic difference.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:39.879
So it's like your microbiomes going from,
from seawater to uh, to soil.

00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:44.120
It's that profound a change compared to different
physical environments, but then between the

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.070
time when you're three and the time when you're
a 90, that's more like save the difference

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:52.750
between one patch of farmland and another,
rather than being a completely different kind

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:53.750
of environment.

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:55.800
So the change is still important, but it's
subtle.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:01.410
And this is the timeframe when a lot of parents
who have little kids know that they get ear

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:03.149
infections, and antibiotics are brought on
board.

00:10:03.149 --> 00:10:05.620
Is this a good thing, a bad thing?

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:07.689
Well, I mean, it is what it is.

00:10:07.689 --> 00:10:08.860
Everything is a trade off.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:14.790
In this particular case, uh, antibiotics as
you've heard and seen, have had an amazingly

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:23.730
important role in saving lives when they were
used for what turned out to be good purposes.

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:27.740
But of course they have collateral damage
that they cause and everything is a, is a

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:28.740
trade off.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.470
So when they're not used for a good reason,
those downsides are gonna outweigh.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:33.470
You.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:35.529
Have you have an opinion on this?

00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:36.529
I do.

00:10:36.529 --> 00:10:41.899
And so, so the, the, the point is as, as we
know, antibiotics are miraculous, uh, when

00:10:41.899 --> 00:10:45.529
they're used appropriately, but they are overused
tremendously.

00:10:45.529 --> 00:10:49.800
The CDC estimated 30 percent of the antibiotics
are overused.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.709
I think the actual number is higher.

00:10:51.709 --> 00:10:56.069
In Sweden they're only using 40 percent of
the antibiotics that we do.

00:10:56.069 --> 00:11:00.579
So it suggests that maybe 60 percent of the
