00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.939
They’re differences in practice and in culture.

00:11:11.939 --> 00:11:15.689
Some people and some doctors want to use antibiotics
more, others don't.

00:11:15.689 --> 00:11:18.589
There doesn't seem to be much difference in
outcome.

00:11:18.589 --> 00:11:22.440
So let's talk a little bit about the human
microbiome project.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:25.129
A lot of people are familiar with the little
petri dishes.

00:11:25.129 --> 00:11:29.639
That's what we think about when we talk about
studying bacteria, but there's a whole new

00:11:29.639 --> 00:11:31.940
way to study bacteria.

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:36.149
Joe, can you sort of walk us through what
that is and how that intersects with human

00:11:36.149 --> 00:11:37.470
microbiome project?

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:43.160
Well thirty years ago, uh, scientists began
to realize that we've been missing a vast

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:48.259
amount of knowledge about the microbiome,
every microbiome, not just in humans but in

00:11:48.259 --> 00:11:51.750
soil and in oceans and on trees.

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:55.251
And the reason that we've been missing that
information is a lot of microbes, the vast

00:11:55.251 --> 00:11:57.920
majority won't grow in culture.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:02.790
So those petri dishes that we all associate
with microbiology are only one little sliver

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:05.199
of the story of microbiology.

00:12:05.199 --> 00:12:10.320
And so the, the effort became to study microbes
without necessarily culturing them and to

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:13.869
circumvent culturing them we started extracting
DNA.

00:12:13.869 --> 00:12:17.990
And so that's the major way that we described
microbiomes today.

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:21.570
And most of the work we'll talk about here
is based on DNA analysis.

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:25.220
So it's not the old fashioned petri dish that's
gone.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:26.339
It's not gone at all.

00:12:26.339 --> 00:12:27.629
Some of it it still is.

00:12:27.629 --> 00:12:32.120
And when we want to study a particular organism
and it can be cultured, that's one of the

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:34.009
most powerful tools we have.

00:12:34.009 --> 00:12:40.579
But if we want to look at the entire community,
including the half or in the case of other

00:12:40.579 --> 00:12:45.209
environments, besides the human system, as
many as 99 percent, that can't be cultured,

00:12:45.209 --> 00:12:47.500
then we need to go to the DNA methods.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:49.649
You've, you've done a lot of work with this.

00:12:49.649 --> 00:12:51.101
Tells us about it.

00:12:51.101 --> 00:12:54.269
And let's take a look at some of what you've
done, if you could describe it.

00:12:54.269 --> 00:12:55.269
Sure.

00:12:55.269 --> 00:12:59.459
So what you're seeing here is my daughter's
microbiome developing over the, over the first

00:12:59.459 --> 00:13:04.290
two and a half years of life, and what you
can see as she's starting off with these different

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:10.749
regions, the gush in Brown, the mouth in blue,
the skin and sorry, the map, in green and

00:13:10.749 --> 00:13:14.999
blue, and she's moving more and more towards
the healthy adult stage as defined by about

00:13:14.999 --> 00:13:19.879
15,000 healthy people in the American Gut
Project, which is our Citizen Science Initiative

00:13:19.879 --> 00:13:23.709
where essentially anyone can get their microbiome
sequenced.

00:13:23.709 --> 00:13:27.439
And so what's exciting about this is even
to a six year old, you can explain how she

00:13:27.439 --> 00:13:29.459
starting off in one place on this map.

00:13:29.459 --> 00:13:35.230
She's moving to another place and what the
regions are little defined by health and disease.

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:38.629
So the different- explained again, the different
colors represent?

00:13:38.629 --> 00:13:42.119
The different colors are the microbiomes in
different parts of the body.

00:13:42.119 --> 00:13:48.019
Each dot is the whole microbiome, so potentially
a thousand microbial species from one individual,

00:13:48.019 --> 00:13:52.600
and we placed two dots on this map close together,
if they're more similar in terms of the evolution

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:54.550
of the microbes that they have in them.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:58.850
We place them further apart, the more dissimilar
in terms of that evolution.

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:02.339
How unique is each person's microbiome?

00:14:02.339 --> 00:14:08.040
um, every, every person’s, microbiome, every
time you sample it, is unique.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:13.110
So if you, if you sample yourself today, three
different times, and you sample yourself tomorrow,

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:16.439
three different times, we can tell those apart
from each other.

00:14:16.439 --> 00:14:18.800
So it's all, it's all a matter of scale.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:23.269
It's about how different are you from yourself,
how different are you from other people and

00:14:23.269 --> 00:14:25.860
which of those differences are large, which
is small.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:29.179
And it's important to remember that you don't
have just one microbiome.

00:14:29.179 --> 00:14:31.579
The microbiome of your mouth, your gut, your
skin.

00:14:31.579 --> 00:14:34.240
They're all completely different from one
another.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:42.440
In fact, Rob's gut a lot more similar to my
gut than, uh, my gut is to my mouth, for example,

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:46.429
Even within the human mouth, there's diversity.

00:14:46.429 --> 00:14:47.600
Is that correct?

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:48.600
Absolutely.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:55.420
A very striking demonstration by Julie Segtr
at the NIH about the different habitats of

00:14:55.420 --> 00:15:01.519
the human skin, and how they're as different
as Rob was saying, as, as grasslands are from

00:15:01.519 --> 00:15:03.180
forest are to deserts.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:09.800
So let's talk about what we've been doing
in the last 40, 50 years.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:11.459
How long has the- 70 years?

00:15:11.459 --> 00:15:12.459
Seventy years.

00:15:12.459 --> 00:15:13.459
That's what.

00:15:13.459 --> 00:15:18.670
That's when antibiotics first came on about
70 years ago, and this is an area of intense

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:19.680
interest to you, Martin.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:25.430
In fact, he wrote a book that's been a compared
to Silent Spring in its importance in terms

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:32.670
of understanding exactly the impact we're
having on our own ecology, if you will, called

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:33.670
“Missing Microbes”.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:38.889
Tell us a little bit about that book, your
thesis and sort of what you think about the

00:15:38.889 --> 00:15:39.889
future.

00:15:39.889 --> 00:15:42.170
Yeah, so it it the.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:46.100
The idea begins with many of the things that
we've discussed before.

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:51.029
The idea that the microbes that we have in
the human body are ancient, that we've inherited

00:15:51.029 --> 00:15:53.810
them from our ancestors, that they're formed.

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:57.149
The structure of our microbiome occurs early
in life.

00:15:57.149 --> 00:16:02.009
That they have benefits to us, they're doing
all kinds of good things, and and now there's

00:16:02.009 --> 00:16:07.230
more and more evidence that our microbiome
is changing, that we have lost diversity,

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:10.839
that we have lost important ancestral microbes.

00:16:10.839 --> 00:16:17.660
And I hypothesized that this has consequence,
and and there's more and more evidence that

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:22.879
the consequences are related to the diseases
that are now becoming epidemic since World

00:16:22.879 --> 00:16:23.879
War II.

00:16:23.879 --> 00:16:29.079
We've had rises in asthma, in obesity and
diabetes, in juvenile diabetes and inflammatory

00:16:29.079 --> 00:16:34.570
bowel disease, and there is an increasing
body of evidence for all of these that the

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:38.449
change in the microbiome is playing some role
in the story.

00:16:38.449 --> 00:16:44.410
You and I were talking beforehand, and you
likened it to other sort of human intervention

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:46.149
impacts like climate change?

00:16:46.149 --> 00:16:54.680
Yeah, so climate change is how humans are
affecting the macroecology through modernization,

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:56.939
through all the things we did.

00:16:56.939 --> 00:17:02.279
When Henry Ford or Benz developed the internal
combustion engine, they didn't anticipate

00:17:02.279 --> 00:17:06.130
that the, every time you turn the ignition,
that the ice cap in Greenland is going to

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:07.680
melt a little.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:12.760
But we think that that's true, and and in
a sense, I think that that's what's happening

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:14.500
in our human bodies.

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:19.290
Every time we take an antibiotic, perhaps
a few organisms become extinct.

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:20.290
And.

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:21.290
Exactly.

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:22.290
Yeah.

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:28.500
That's kind of change in our micro ecology
is paralleling what we’re doing in our macro

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:29.500
ecology.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:33.910
You also said you think what's happening to
our microbiome is happening much, much faster.

00:17:33.910 --> 00:17:34.910
Yeah.

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:39.940
Well, we're 70 years into the antibiotic era
and antibiotics are not the only factor.

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:40.940
C-section.

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:44.920
There are many other factors involved, but
maybe it's, it’s the most important.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:47.320
And so it's a difficult problem.

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:48.440
Antibiotics are miraculous.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:53.840
They save lives, but we're overusing them,
so we have to, we have to dial it back and

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:58.470
we have to understand what we're doing, and
then how we can restore what we've lost.

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:00.850
Dirt is good, right?

00:18:00.850 --> 00:18:02.230
Title of your book.

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:03.530
Tell us why dirt’s good.

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:09.120
It's good because exposures of a lot of natural
microbes in the environment, whether it's

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:14.770
from dirt or whether it's from animals in
farms, or healthy, healthy water, healthy

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:21.600
soil and so forth has surprising number of
beneficial effects, and it can give the microbial

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:26.980
diversity that we need to reduce the, reduce
the frequency of some of these conditions.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:30.640
Although it's an open question, how much we
can restore, like I think much more direct

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:37.100
analogy with antibiotics is DDT where it saved
millions of lives and yet you have this hugely

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:42.290
deleterious effect on the ecosystem and then
afterwards just introducing a few rats and

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:46.790
pigeons isn't really going to cut it for improving
biodiversity again.

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:47.790
Right?

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:52.170
So understanding what we're changing and what
we need to do to restore is really key scientific

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:53.830
challenge at the moment.

00:18:53.830 --> 00:19:00.030
In the meantime, if you're a parent and the
pacifier hits the sidewalk, do you boil it?

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:02.650
Do you call in the Hazmat team?

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:03.650
Yeah.

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:06.070
Well it depends a bit where the sidewalk is.

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:09.630
Like if you, if you think there is a lot of
lead, maybe you shouldn't, maybe you should

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:14.190
worry about, but it's more a more chemical
than a microbiological segment you should

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:15.370
worry about.

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:20.840
So if it drops on a hospital floor, you probably
want to wash that off, but if it's your floor

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:25.180
at home or if you don't have any reason to
believe that there’s anything bad there,

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:27.760
probably the positive effect outweighs the
negative.

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:31.700
Any, any opinions here on the six second rule?

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:34.860
For some reason the NIH hasn’t funded a
lot of research.

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:35.860
I'm,

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.740
Interesting, big problems.

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:44.190
We, um, let's talk about some of the specific
diseases that we, we've been alluding to.

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:46.170
The first is asthma.

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:48.520
Asthma is a major public health issue.

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:51.890
If you're in New York City, there are certain
neighborhoods in New York City that are truly

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:53.370
plagued with asthma.

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:59.770
Overall eight percent of Americans have asthma,
but we've looked at these sort of ecological

00:19:59.770 --> 00:20:06.700
studies, and the stories actually vary kind
of stark when you look at very particular

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:07.700
communities.

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:12.020
Two of the communities in a recent article
in the New England Journal of Medicine looked

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:15.020
at the Amish and the Hutterites.

00:20:15.020 --> 00:20:17.420
Why those communities and what did we learn
from them?

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:21.600
Yeah, so this was what from Jack Gilbert and
Erika von Mutius have found in a certain number

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:27.280
of their collaborations, and what's amazing
is that the Amish and the Hutterites both

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:32.210
live what we would consider relatively traditional
lifestyles, very fun based, but they have

00:20:32.210 --> 00:20:36.380
completely different rates of asthma where
the Amish have essentially no asthma and the

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:38.110
Hutterites have a lot.

00:20:38.110 --> 00:20:42.620
And what's different is that the Amish will
take their kids on the farms to interact with

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:47.720
the animals, whereas the Hutterites have a
very, have a very tight distinction between

00:20:47.720 --> 00:20:51.710
farm life and home life, and they make sure
that they change their clothes and take their

00:20:51.710 --> 00:20:53.950
kids out of the farm and so on.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:59.320
And the exposure to particular kinds of microbes
including some kinds of lactobacillus seems

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:03.220
to be really important for the difference
in health between the two communities.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:08.900
The Amish were six times less likely to have
asthma and allergies than the hutterites,

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:14.570
and the Hutterites have asthma rates that
are consistent with the rest of the country.

00:21:14.570 --> 00:21:20.750
Does this, does this go towards what they
call the hygiene hypothesis for the development

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:21.840
of diseases?

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:22.840
Can anyone?

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:23.840
That's right.

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:28.270
So, so the, the first iteration of the hygiene
hypothesis was the idea that exposure to childhood

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:32.140
pathogens was good, and there hasn't been
a lot of evidence in favor of that.

00:21:32.140 --> 00:21:36.090
What we're thinking now is much more along
the lines of it’s exposure to those diverse

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:39.290
array of beneficial microbes from the environment,
that’s good.

00:21:39.290 --> 00:21:42.960
But by trying too, trying too hard to keep
yourself away from the pathogens, you may

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:45.400
also be walling yourself off from those beneficial
microbes.

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:47.060
David, did you want to add to that?

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:50.990
No, I think I mean a lot of this, as Martin
was saying, it's just a function of what we

00:21:50.990 --> 00:21:53.850
have been doing both to ourselves and to our
environment.

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:58.980
It's a function in part of the built-in environment
now we have segregated ourselves from the

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:02.330
environments we used to, to be born and raised
in

00:22:02.330 --> 00:22:08.830
And it's striking that kids traditionally
have liked to eat soil, and a lot of animals

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:09.830
eat soil.

00:22:09.830 --> 00:22:14.020
In fact, when they're sick, some animals self
medicate with soil, so it seems like there's

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:19.220
some natural process of ingesting soil, and,
and maybe we're learning why that, that was

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:21.090
an impulse for so many kids.

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:25.860
In some ways when you look at those kids,
what they do and what we all do, it's really

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:33.860
also about continuing to sample the, our environment
in order to find those missing microbes, right?

00:22:33.860 --> 00:22:39.480
That, that, that can fill in the niches that,
and habitats that had been emptied.

00:22:39.480 --> 00:22:43.460
And so in some ways that natural behavior
is a really a very adaptive one.

00:22:43.460 --> 00:22:47.920
I know you guys are working on some additional
information about this?

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:48.920
Yeah.

00:22:48.920 --> 00:22:53.060
We're working on a new study with Erica in
Bavaria where, uh, where you have, you can

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:57.370
go down a street and you'll have houses that
have cows where they bring the cow into the

00:22:57.370 --> 00:22:59.690
barn versus houses that don't have cows.

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:03.850
And even on that level, one house to the next
on the street, if you have a cow, you're much

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:07.600
less likely to have asthma than if you don't
have a cow.

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:14.060
Now obviously in New York, recommending that
every parent get a cow for their kid is not

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:15.060
particularly helpful.

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:18.190
So, so this is an ongoing going study that.

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:19.190
Okay.

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:26.020
All right, so we, so that's asthma, but I
think even more interesting than asthma is

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:33.220
obesity and um, you know, we all know that
obesity is an epidemic in this country.

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:40.080
This is not news, but Martin you have looked
at, we've overlapped some information and

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:42.990
what you're talking about in terms of the
obesity epidemic and some of the

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:49.910
So, so there are many things to say but I,
I don't want to take up the whole time, but

00:23:49.910 --> 00:23:53.290
you know one, one of the most important observations,

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:55.170
These are the rising of the obesity rates.

00:23:55.170 --> 00:24:01.920
These are data from the CDC and the darker
the color, the more, the higher the percentage

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:02.920
of obesity.

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:07.150
So you can see the difference between 1989
and 1999 and 2010.

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:11.090
So this is only 21 years, something very important
has happened.

00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:16.920
It's happened all over the country and about
2012 we made the connection.

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:22.670
I think it's in the next slide that if you
look at the, if you look at the obesity map

00:24:22.670 --> 00:24:27.320
and the regional variation in antibiotic use,
there's a lot of similarity.

00:24:27.320 --> 00:24:29.990
There was a lot of overlap, so that's it.

00:24:29.990 --> 00:24:30.990
That's kind of interesting.

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:36.630
It doesn't prove anything because it's observational,
but 70 years ago farmers found that if they

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:40.340
gave antibiotics to their farm animals, it
would change their growth.

00:24:40.340 --> 00:24:44.540
They would grow bigger, they'd grow faster,
it changed their metabolism, and they found

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:48.680
that the younger they gave the antibiotics,
the more profound the effect, so we thought

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:52.470
that maybe that's one of the things that’s
filling, and we began to do experiments in

00:24:52.470 --> 00:25:01.170
mice as with others in which we could give
antibiotics to mice as the only variable and

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:02.760
that increased their obesity.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:04.300
Jo, you've done work on that.

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:08.920
Can you take us through what these mouse experiments
where

00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:15.020
Yeah, this isn't my own work, but other people
have done obesity experiments where they transfer

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:20.790
the microbes from obese humans into mice and
they see the mice are gaining weight quite

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:21.790
dramatically.

00:25:21.790 --> 00:25:26.820
I think this was one of the turning points
in the microbiome research field because we

00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:31.920
began to see cause and effect, as Martin said,
the those maps show you great epidemiological

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:36.910
similarities and correlations, but this was
a direct effect by transferring microbes.

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:38.120
It's, it's amazing.

00:25:38.120 --> 00:25:40.300
I mean, how, how could this work?

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:47.470
Is anyone working on what is the mechanism,
the elixir, the, the genetic turn that happens

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:48.470
here?

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:49.470
we and others are working on.

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:51.510
It's not genetic, it's environmental.

00:25:51.510 --> 00:25:56.240
It's, because the obesity epidemic is too
fast to be a genetic problem.

00:25:56.240 --> 00:25:57.900
It's not taking multiple generations.

00:25:57.900 --> 00:25:59.700
It's happening in years.

00:25:59.700 --> 00:26:04.720
And again, it goes back to the early years
of life when the microbiome is forming.

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:06.840
It's educating our immune system.

00:26:06.840 --> 00:26:08.290
It's educating metabolism.

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:13.890
It’s educating the liver and the fat cells
on how, how big to be and how much, how much

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:17.170
energy to store and how much to save.

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:22.820
And it's very, it's, it's very conserved because
the farmers found that it works in cows, but

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:24.140
also works in chickens.

00:26:24.140 --> 00:26:28.350
And that's a big, big swath in vertebrate
evolution.

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:34.340
Is there a, is there any, um, knowledge about
exactly what the microbes are doing that lead

00:26:34.340 --> 00:26:35.340
to?

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:37.670
There are many things that are going on at
the same time.

00:26:37.670 --> 00:26:40.940
It's, it's changing how the microbes use energy.

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:47.140
It's also changing how the microbes are signaling
human cells at this critical early time of

00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:48.140
development.

00:26:48.140 --> 00:26:52.980
And that's also a strong correlation between
the inflammatory response and many chronic

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:58.480
diseases and so the bacteria are in direct
contact with our gut and they can incite an

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:03.260
immune response or inflammatory response,
and there's some evidence that reducing that

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:09.390
inflammatory response with food or other additives
to the Diet, including microbes may be one

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:11.060
of the ways we could treat obesity.

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:17.141
I would, I would just add a note of, in my
view as as slight caution, but actually I

00:27:17.141 --> 00:27:19.910
think it's incredibly exciting.

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:26.290
The idea that these communities, like any
of the communities, are amazingly adaptable,

00:27:26.290 --> 00:27:34.311
so they will recognize conditions those members
and and interacting parts of the community

00:27:34.311 --> 00:27:37.950
that are favored will flourish and the others
not.

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:44.710
So essentially these communities remodel themselves
under whatever condition they are presented.

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:53.450
In this way, they become almost a reinforcing
factor for whatever the host or human disease

00:27:53.450 --> 00:28:00.540
or health state might be, and I think in part
that may be why the transfer of a gut community

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:08.560
that's now adapted to a state of obesity will
bring with it the metabolism and the and the

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:11.770
biology that it took for them to adapt.

00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:14.400
They bring it to that new host, but it's just.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:19.720
It's a piece of the story and it's a caution
in the sense that the microbial community

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:21.560
is not the whole story.

00:28:21.560 --> 00:28:29.750
It could be a reflection of, as well as a
driver of, or a reinforcer of whatever the

00:28:29.750 --> 00:28:30.750
host problem might be.

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:31.750
And especially with human obesity.

00:28:31.750 --> 00:28:34.850
There's a lot of complex moving parts there.

00:28:34.850 --> 00:28:40.160
Even in mice, though, one thing we haven't
heard yet about is behavior, and for example

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:46.570
in the toll-like receptor 5 knockout mice
that we studied with Andrew T. Gewirtz and

00:28:46.570 --> 00:28:50.280
Ruth Ley, what was amazing they are is that
you have a genetic change in the mouse that

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:53.090
causes, that causes microbial change.

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:57.350
But you can transmit from one mouse to another,
transmit obesity, and what was going on there

00:28:57.350 --> 00:28:58.610
was it was almost all behavioral.

00:28:58.610 --> 00:29:01.900
So the ultimate microbes made the mice eat
more.

00:29:01.900 --> 00:29:03.710
That's why they became obese.

00:29:03.710 --> 00:29:06.980
You can make them lean again by just cutting
off their food supply to what a normal mouse

00:29:06.980 --> 00:29:08.770
would eat.

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:12.720
And so these connections between the gut and
the brain are really exciting, even in the

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:16.680
context of things we might think of as more
physical, like obesity.

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:19.060
Marty, you said it's not genetic, right?

00:29:19.060 --> 00:29:23.090
That it, that this phenomenon that we're seeing
is not genetic.

00:29:23.090 --> 00:29:29.850
And yet you have also said that certain conditions
like inflammatory bowel disease may actually

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:32.450
be transmissible to the next generation.

00:29:32.450 --> 00:29:34.920
So how does that work if it's not genetic?

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:41.020
Well, um, you know, when we classically say
genetics, we're talking about our human genes

00:29:41.020 --> 00:29:42.670
passed from generation to generation.

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:47.910
But of course we're also passing our microbial
genes from generation to generation when,

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:52.760
when, when we're born, it's, it's not quite
as precise as the humans, but there's still

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:55.410
a lot of inheritance of the microbial genes.

00:29:55.410 --> 00:30:00.550
And we've, we've done experiments to look
at this particular question.

00:30:00.550 --> 00:30:04.950
You've done experiments in mice and this should,
what are we looking at here?

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:05.950
Yeah.

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:14.470
So, so we're looking at the colon of two mice,
uh, on the left side are, we'll call that

00:30:14.470 --> 00:30:16.150
the normal, right as the perturbed.

00:30:16.150 --> 00:30:23.500
So did the experiment we did here is that
we took poop essentially from mice that were

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:26.500
either normal or had received antibiotics.

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:29.620
So we had a perturbed poop and a normal poop.

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:35.930
And we gave that to germ-free mice, mice that
had no flora who happened to be pregnant,

00:30:35.930 --> 00:30:40.180
and who also were immune-deficient, they were
IL-10 deficient mice.

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:43.600
These are mice that spontaneously develop
colitis.

00:30:43.600 --> 00:30:49.110
And so now we gave the poop to the, to the
moms either the normal, the perturbed.

00:30:49.110 --> 00:30:54.060
Then we asked would the mom transfer the microbes
to the, to her pups.

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:57.120
She did, we followed the pups till they were
middle aged.

00:30:57.120 --> 00:30:59.770
And then when we sacrificed them, we looked
at their colon.

00:30:59.770 --> 00:31:06.190
And what we found is that the mice whose mothers
got the perturbed poop, they had much worse

00:31:06.190 --> 00:31:07.550
inflammation.

00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:09.230
That's the panel on the right.

00:31:09.230 --> 00:31:14.050
Had much worse inflammation in their colon
than the than the other, so there was a huge

00:31:14.050 --> 00:31:15.050
difference.

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:19.630
And the point here is that those pups never
saw an antibiotic.

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:21.480
Their mother never saw an antibiotic.

00:31:21.480 --> 00:31:23.220
All they saw was a perturbed poop.

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:29.050
So this was evidence that the signal, that
the disease signal is being transferred by

00:31:29.050 --> 00:31:37.070
the microbiome, and it also is evidence that
the inheritance is also involving microbiome,

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:39.230
involving microbial genes, not host genes,

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:40.230
Right?

00:31:40.230 --> 00:31:41.960
So not host genes, microbial genes.

00:31:41.960 --> 00:31:46.320
What are the implications of something like
this?

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:52.640
One implication is that many of the diseases
that we think are genetic, it may be more

00:31:52.640 --> 00:31:55.430
than human genetics, for example, inflammatory
bowel disease.

00:31:55.430 --> 00:32:01.140
If you take all of the genetic low side that
have been found to be associated with inflammatory

00:32:01.140 --> 00:32:04.300
bowel disease, that explains 10 percent of
the risk.

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:06.530
The other 90 percent was unexplained.

00:32:06.530 --> 00:32:08.250
I think this is part of it.

00:32:08.250 --> 00:32:13.630
It also has kind of profound social implications
because it means that we can pass our experience

00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:15.170
onto our children.

00:32:15.170 --> 00:32:18.770
You know, Lamarck was sort of debunked a long
time ago.

00:32:18.770 --> 00:32:22.710
The concept that we acquire traits during
our lifetime and then pass them on.

00:32:22.710 --> 00:32:27.030
In other words, if you lift weights a lot,
your kids will get big muscles.

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:28.030
Exactly right.

00:32:28.030 --> 00:32:32.370
And, and you know, we, we don't believe that,
that sounds silly, but on the other hand,

00:32:32.370 --> 00:32:37.730
if we're passing on our microbes and they
are a product of our existence and during

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:43.380
our lifetime and not just our own genes, then
it really has almost a, uh, and I, I think

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:50.120
almost anti democratic quality to it, that
it says that the old adage of pulling ourselves

00:32:50.120 --> 00:32:55.590
up by our bootstraps and creating our own
lives and every life is a new life and has

00:32:55.590 --> 00:32:59.130
the fresh start with all the opportunities
just simply isn't true.

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:02.290
We are literally inheriting our parents' experience.

00:33:02.290 --> 00:33:03.290
Wow.

00:33:03.290 --> 00:33:07.500
This brings us, this is good point to talk
about antibiotic, antibiotic resistance.

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:13.020
Before we, before we get to that, we want
to talk about the bad boy of anti-resistant

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:20.980
bugs, one of the baddest boys, and that is
C. difficile, which causes untold miserable

00:33:20.980 --> 00:33:23.300
outcomes in many, many people.

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:31.100
And the CDC has turned this a very urgent
