00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:34.350
treatment for this and we're going to talk
about it in this video.

00:33:34.350 --> 00:33:36.950
I was seeing urologist for chronic pelvic
pain.

00:33:36.950 --> 00:33:39.840
They would just prescribe me an antibiotic
after antibiotic.

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:46.350
The antibiotics cleared up the infection causing
his pain, but then Ryan began to get different

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:47.350
symptoms.

00:33:47.350 --> 00:33:51.110
I finally found out that I had C. diff.

00:33:51.110 --> 00:33:56.700
C. diff or C. difficile is an opportunistic
microbe that often afflicts those whose microbiomes

00:33:56.700 --> 00:33:59.510
have been compromised by antibiotics.

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:04.620
It's notoriously difficult to treat and makes
day-to-day life miserable.

00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:06.250
C. diff can be life threatening.

00:34:06.250 --> 00:34:07.940
People can die of this.

00:34:07.940 --> 00:34:10.409
None of the medications help with my symptoms.

00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:17.290
It’s just been really draining having this
disease for so long.

00:34:17.290 --> 00:34:19.710
I have a disease I know is due to biome disruption.

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:20.710
Let's fix the biome.

00:34:20.710 --> 00:34:22.720
Easy to say, challenging to do.

00:34:22.720 --> 00:34:23.720
The treatment.

00:34:23.720 --> 00:34:25.760
It's called a fecal transplant.

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:29.840
The check there was FMT, fecal microbiota
transplantation.

00:34:29.840 --> 00:34:32.280
It's exactly what it sounds like.

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:40.800
Someone with a healthy microbiome has donated
his stool to be placed in Ryan's colon.

00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:43.450
We drive our camera to the top of the colon.

00:34:43.450 --> 00:34:48.050
We squeezed in some biome, scored it in with
a syringe.

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:50.320
That's exactly how elegant it or not elegant
that is.

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:56.419
Inside of the stool, there are trillions of
bacteria that when transplanted actually outcompete

00:34:56.419 --> 00:34:58.460
the seeded bacteria.

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:00.030
And that repopulates you.

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:07.610
Soon after the procedure, Ryan was free from
C. diff.

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:09.390
How does that work?

00:35:09.390 --> 00:35:11.990
Did you.

00:35:11.990 --> 00:35:13.730
Is this more than you bargained for?

00:35:13.730 --> 00:35:15.410
Is everybody okay?

00:35:15.410 --> 00:35:24.300
Well, I mean the good news is that it's been
almost a miracle for people like this patient

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:27.010
here and many others.

00:35:27.010 --> 00:35:31.540
It's remarkable in fact that it, that it works
at least in this circumstance.

00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:39.190
And the ideas that in the wake of this very
inflammatory, destructive infection caused

00:35:39.190 --> 00:35:48.550
by C. difficile and its toxins, the habitat
is damaged and yet in some ways unstable and

00:35:48.550 --> 00:35:55.530
receptive for a new community to replace what
is now sort of the ravaged remains of what

00:35:55.530 --> 00:36:02.540
might be there, and in that particular setting,
the, the provision of a new community, it's

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:10.200
really restoration ecology allows the habitat
to now be filled by a community type that

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:15.610
really was destined, in some ways, to be in
that environment when the inflammation has

00:36:15.610 --> 00:36:16.610
subsided.

00:36:16.610 --> 00:36:22.460
So it's a very particular circumstance, and
I think a very important lesson about what

00:36:22.460 --> 00:36:24.520
we ought to be working towards.

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:29.220
Not necessarily, you know, poop as is, but
next generation.

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:30.220
Restoration.

00:36:30.220 --> 00:36:34.310
Well the good news is you can get the poop
