different, the degrees and ways in which they
interact with other humans are different.

00:47:35.150 --> 00:47:41.030
They take medications, and so all of those
things we know can independently affect a

00:47:41.030 --> 00:47:42.030
microbiome.

00:47:42.030 --> 00:47:47.280
That microbiome in turn may now turn into
a reflection of all of those factors given

00:47:47.280 --> 00:47:55.360
to a mouse may not be so surprising to induce
changes that we read as similar, but again,

00:47:55.360 --> 00:48:02.040
the actual causation and multiple contributions
of many factors make this much more complicated,

00:48:02.040 --> 00:48:05.480
much harder to solve unfortunately then that
might suggests.

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:06.890
Than in the mice.

00:48:06.890 --> 00:48:12.349
Yeah, so David is emphasizing a very important
note of caution.

00:48:12.349 --> 00:48:17.030
I think we're all here because we're enthusiasts
about the microbiome.

00:48:17.030 --> 00:48:22.170
We recognize that it is a scientific frontier
just like many scientific frontiers that's

00:48:22.170 --> 00:48:27.900
going to bear a lot of fruit one day, but
maybe not tomorrow because it's going to take

00:48:27.900 --> 00:48:33.230
time to translate, and to understand all these
new findings, and to convert them into new

00:48:33.230 --> 00:48:36.750
approaches to health and treatment.

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:37.750
It's not.

00:48:37.750 --> 00:48:41.599
It's not coming tomorrow, except for C. diff.

00:48:41.599 --> 00:48:46.250
Sometimes it takes only one study to take
a gigantic step forward.

00:48:46.250 --> 00:48:50.390
I thought that the brain science that that
people are doing now with the microbiome would

00:48:50.390 --> 00:48:55.870
take forever to begin to sort out what's really
going on in those mouse brains and then just

00:48:55.870 --> 00:49:00.910
last month the paper came out that showed
anatomical changes in the amygdala, which

00:49:00.910 --> 00:49:09.650
is the part of the brain that controls feeling
and depression to some degree, and if you

00:49:09.650 --> 00:49:13.450
changed the microbiome in a mouse, you changed
the structure of the Amygdala.

00:49:13.450 --> 00:49:18.010
Now that's starting to get to a structural
level and I think ultimately it will be a

00:49:18.010 --> 00:49:20.440
biochemical level where we can really dissect
it.

00:49:20.440 --> 00:49:25.540
So sometimes it just takes a very innovative
study to take that giant next step and get

00:49:25.540 --> 00:49:27.550
down to the mechanism of what's going on.

00:49:27.550 --> 00:49:34.480
It’s early times, but would you agree that
there are hints that there is a strong strong

00:49:34.480 --> 00:49:38.610
association between what's happening in the
gut and behavior?

00:49:38.610 --> 00:49:44.400
Rob, I think you've done some work in PTSD
or you're familiar with this?

00:49:44.400 --> 00:49:50.090
Yeah, well, well again, again, muscles mostly
in mouse models, but with Chris Larry at Boulder

00:49:50.090 --> 00:49:55.520
and a number of others, we were able to show
that if you expose mice to the right kinds

00:49:55.520 --> 00:50:01.930
of soil bacteria, an organism called Mycobacterium
vaccae, then you can put it into social stress

00:50:01.930 --> 00:50:02.930
conditions.

00:50:02.930 --> 00:50:06.460
So basically you either put it in with a huge
dominant male mouse that would beat it up

00:50:06.460 --> 00:50:08.640
or you just let it watch it.

00:50:08.640 --> 00:50:10.950
Watch that through glass, which also upsets
them, right?

00:50:10.950 --> 00:50:15.710
And say normally they'll hover in the corner
of the cage, they won't eat, their hair falls

00:50:15.710 --> 00:50:19.140
out, but if you inoculate them with these
soil bacteria, they're very resilient against

00:50:19.140 --> 00:50:20.490
that stress.

00:50:20.490 --> 00:50:25.630
And we also did another study looking at looking
at children with PTSD where there were some

00:50:25.630 --> 00:50:29.510
microbiome differences between those with
and without p, uh, PTSD.

00:50:29.510 --> 00:50:31.270
But again, we don't have causality in that.

00:50:31.270 --> 00:50:32.850
We just have association.

00:50:32.850 --> 00:50:39.130
So, so, so the, so the concept that you can
modify the mouse’s behavior in response

00:50:39.130 --> 00:50:42.340
to the social stress by, by adding a microbe.

00:50:42.340 --> 00:50:45.840
That's very exciting, but it takes a long
time to translate that to something that you

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:47.070
could do in humans.

00:50:47.070 --> 00:50:50.770
And, and you've even looked at mice with autism.

00:50:50.770 --> 00:50:53.000
Is this correct and?

00:50:53.000 --> 00:50:57.700
We haven't, but Sarkis Mazmanian at Caltech
has done that and so what's,

00:50:57.700 --> 00:50:59.630
What is this we are seeing here?

00:50:59.630 --> 00:51:01.180
That's the marble burying test.

00:51:01.180 --> 00:51:05.330
So one of the hallmarks of autism as compulsive
behavior and that's a mouse that can't help

00:51:05.330 --> 00:51:06.790
burying marbles.

00:51:06.790 --> 00:51:07.790
And the reason that-

00:51:07.790 --> 00:51:10.980
A mouse that likes to bury marbles?

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:11.980
Correct.

00:51:11.980 --> 00:51:15.770
So the number of marbles that there is in
a fixed amount of time, it is a measure of

00:51:15.770 --> 00:51:16.770
how much compulsive behavior there is-

00:51:16.770 --> 00:51:18.370
Is this repetitive behavior?

00:51:18.370 --> 00:51:20.440
Exactly like, like the mouse version of OCD.

00:51:20.440 --> 00:51:21.440
Interesting.

00:51:21.440 --> 00:51:22.440
Okay.

00:51:22.440 --> 00:51:23.440
So-

00:51:23.440 --> 00:51:28.910
And so, so what happens is when you, when
you inject the pregnant female mice with a

00:51:28.910 --> 00:51:33.440
double stranded RNA, so you simulate a viral
attack, and then the pups do that.

00:51:33.440 --> 00:51:37.910
They have communications deficits, they have
cognitive deficits, and they have problems

00:51:37.910 --> 00:51:41.599
with their GI tract, all of which resemble
human autism.

00:51:41.599 --> 00:51:47.640
And so what, what Sarkis showed was that this
molecule called 4-ethylphenyl sulfate that’s

00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:53.310
overproduced in the microbiomes of those pups,
that if you inject a normal pup worth it,

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:55.140
it'll do many of the same things.

00:51:55.140 --> 00:51:59.750
You can also use the natural microbiota of
the human gut as a probiotic to reverse some

00:51:59.750 --> 00:52:00.750
of those effects.

00:52:00.750 --> 00:52:02.930
So, so again, this is all pretty clinical.

00:52:02.930 --> 00:52:07.140
There was another consortium of three groups
in ours that I know did a really exciting

00:52:07.140 --> 00:52:11.400
clinical trial that was published a little
over a year ago with a small number of kids

00:52:11.400 --> 00:52:14.020
doing fecal transplantation for autism.

00:52:14.020 --> 00:52:21.080
And so they saw some benefits both in GI dysfunction
and also in cognitive function in those children.

00:52:21.080 --> 00:52:22.900
But it's still very early days, right?

00:52:22.900 --> 00:52:27.690
It's a small open label trial and before you
believe that it's going to be the next thing,

00:52:27.690 --> 00:52:28.690
likeC.

00:52:28.690 --> 00:52:32.640
diff that can be addressed with fecal transplant,
you really want to see a lot more research?

00:52:32.640 --> 00:52:34.340
But it's very encouraging.

00:52:34.340 --> 00:52:40.440
Has anyone looked at populations that have
not used intensive antibiotics the way we

00:52:40.440 --> 00:52:46.600
have in developed countries, and what their
microbiomes are like compared to an American's

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:47.640
microbiome?

00:52:47.640 --> 00:52:53.090
So Rob and I have been involved in several
studies that have looked at that very question

00:52:53.090 --> 00:53:00.150
and the people living out in the jungles,
in the bush, who have not had much modernization,

00:53:00.150 --> 00:53:02.520
their microbiomes are much richer than ours.

00:53:02.520 --> 00:53:07.920
They're much more diverse, and that these
are people who live in Africa and South America

00:53:07.920 --> 00:53:09.210
and New Guinea.

00:53:09.210 --> 00:53:11.180
They're genetically different.

00:53:11.180 --> 00:53:16.180
They're on all different diets, but what they
share in common is that they have very rich

00:53:16.180 --> 00:53:24.080
microbiota, and the people who live in Italy,
Australia, US, different continents, different

00:53:24.080 --> 00:53:31.960
ethnicities, et Cetera, Japan, but we, we
share modernization and industrialization.

00:53:31.960 --> 00:53:35.170
Our microbiota are less rich.

00:53:35.170 --> 00:53:36.990
does that have an implication on their health?

00:53:36.990 --> 00:53:42.619
Are they, are they in terms of the chronic
diseases we've talked about today, asthma,

00:53:42.619 --> 00:53:44.790
obesity, some of the-

00:53:44.790 --> 00:53:50.680
Those comparisons are different, are difficult
because we are different from the people living

00:53:50.680 --> 00:53:54.540
in the jungles in many different ways, but
in general, they have less asthma, and they

00:53:54.540 --> 00:53:59.380
have less obesity but that it's hard to prove
things from that, but what we can say is that

00:53:59.380 --> 00:54:05.869
they have a much richer microbiota and it
suggests that theirs is more typical, is more

00:54:05.869 --> 00:54:11.580
similar to our ancestral microbiota and that
we have lost diversity.

00:54:11.580 --> 00:54:17.210
So if I understand this correctly, you're
saying that it's possible we cannot recapture

00:54:17.210 --> 00:54:22.540
that diversity that we most likely started
with some of the.

00:54:22.540 --> 00:54:24.109
I don't think we want to say.

00:54:24.109 --> 00:54:26.990
We certainly don't want
to believe that.

00:54:26.990 --> 00:54:33.060
We need first of course, to understand what
parts of that ancestral diversity, if you

00:54:33.060 --> 00:54:36.300
will, are important to us today.

00:54:36.300 --> 00:54:37.500
In some ways.

00:54:37.500 --> 00:54:43.050
Of course you don't want the Hadza’s microbiome
in order to be able to survive in New York

00:54:43.050 --> 00:54:44.050
today.

00:54:44.050 --> 00:54:45.050
The who?

00:54:45.050 --> 00:54:49.670
The Hadza was, the one of the ancestral, the
hunter gatherer populations.

00:54:49.670 --> 00:54:52.770
That would not be well suited to modern day
life.

00:54:52.770 --> 00:54:57.750
But in turn, we ought to be thinking about
what elements of it should we attempt to restore?

00:54:57.750 --> 00:55:00.840
What functional aspects can we restore?

00:55:00.840 --> 00:55:07.450
Can we restore and should we restore in order
to improve long, long-term outcomes and avoid

00:55:07.450 --> 00:55:10.290
some of the calamities of modern life, if
that's

00:55:10.290 --> 00:55:18.140
Rob and I are involved in a new foundation
that my wife, Maria Gloria Dominguez, uh,

00:55:18.140 --> 00:55:25.020
is establishing to create a microbiome vault
similar to the seed vault that is in Norway

00:55:25.020 --> 00:55:26.970
to preserve all the seeds of humankind.

00:55:26.970 --> 00:55:31.450
You guys familiar with the seed vault?

00:55:31.450 --> 00:55:44.471
So the same idea is to archive those ancestral
microbiota now before they're lost, so that

00:55:44.471 --> 00:55:50.500
at least we have the resource so that if there
will be, if the restoration will be feasible,

00:55:50.500 --> 00:55:53.820
we will have the materials for the restoration.

00:55:53.820 --> 00:56:00.280
We don't know yet whether it will be feasible
or how, uh, I'm optimistic, but it may, it

00:56:00.280 --> 00:56:04.230
may take some time to figure out what are
the rules, what are the ground rules?

00:56:04.230 --> 00:56:08.839
Is, is, is the restoration of my children
going to be the same as the restoration for

00:56:08.839 --> 00:56:10.150
your children?

00:56:10.150 --> 00:56:12.510
I think for us, maybe we're.

00:56:12.510 --> 00:56:13.541
It's a little late.

00:56:13.541 --> 00:56:17.400
It's a little late.

00:56:17.400 --> 00:56:20.060
It's not so much the crop trust was the crap
trust, if you will.

00:56:20.060 --> 00:56:26.349
But we really need to preserve this material
now when we have the opportunity,

00:56:26.349 --> 00:56:27.349
Right?

00:56:27.349 --> 00:56:33.660
Well, we've spent a lot of time sort of talking
about the ways that we've hurt ourselves and

00:56:33.660 --> 00:56:38.560
our own microbiome over the last decades,
if you will, with antibiotics and other things.

00:56:38.560 --> 00:56:40.810
Let's try to end on a positive note.

00:56:40.810 --> 00:56:48.440
Let's talk about ways we can enhance or improve
or change things going forward.

00:56:48.440 --> 00:56:51.609
How can we keep our microbiome happy?

00:56:51.609 --> 00:56:53.230
What should we be doing?

00:56:53.230 --> 00:56:56.700
Rob, you talk about getting outside.

00:56:56.700 --> 00:56:57.840
Tell us a little bit about that.

00:56:57.840 --> 00:56:58.840
Give us some advice.

00:56:58.840 --> 00:57:05.350
How can we be, um, moving forward for ourselves
and our kids and maybe maybe turn things around

00:57:05.350 --> 00:57:06.480
a little bit here.

00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:07.480
Sure.

00:57:07.480 --> 00:57:11.530
Well, the most important thing you can do
for your gut microbiome based on what we see

00:57:11.530 --> 00:57:15.770
in the, in the American Gut Project is eating
a wide range of different plants.

00:57:15.770 --> 00:57:20.920
So that seems to matter a lot more than if
you sit down to a huge plate of kale or if

00:57:20.920 --> 00:57:26.060
you sometimes eat meat, but that diversity
in plants also seems to be really important.

00:57:26.060 --> 00:57:28.500
So, so okay.

00:57:28.500 --> 00:57:32.720
Also, when it comes to particular foods, what
types of foods should we be eating

00:57:32.720 --> 00:57:38.450
Well, so at sort of general level, you should
avoid sweet and salty snacks, but I think

00:57:38.450 --> 00:57:39.450
you knew that already.

00:57:39.450 --> 00:57:44.869
One of the things that's coming out in the
microbiome, is this amazing individual variability

00:57:44.869 --> 00:57:48.730
that explains why different people respond
so differently to changes in diet, so the

00:57:48.730 --> 00:57:54.220
science isn't quite there yet to give personalized
recommendations, but it's getting pretty close

00:57:54.220 --> 00:57:57.940
and in the context of individual research
studies, that's working pretty well now.

00:57:57.940 --> 00:58:03.650
But don't we know that insoluble fiber, for
example, increases butyrate-producing bacteria,

00:58:03.650 --> 00:58:07.210
and butyrate is a pretty important part of
gut health?

00:58:07.210 --> 00:58:08.210
Absolutely.

00:58:08.210 --> 00:58:12.770
That's really good for most people, but if
you have IBD, it's a significant problem,

00:58:12.770 --> 00:58:16.869
and eating fiber it's really bad, so that's
why it's always challenging to give generalized

00:58:16.869 --> 00:58:20.310
recommendations that work for everyone, but
there was one Israeli.

00:58:20.310 --> 00:58:25.200
There was one Israeli study in particular
from Eran Segal and Eran Elinav at Weizmann

00:58:25.200 --> 00:58:29.320
Institute a couple of years ago where when
they look to their population, they found

00:58:29.320 --> 00:58:33.220
for a fairly sizable fraction of people, it
was actually better for their blood sugar

00:58:33.220 --> 00:58:37.849
to eat ice cream than it was to eat rice,
and they never expected that rational.

00:58:37.849 --> 00:58:43.230
When you find that out, you might wonder is
there a test I can do to find out if I'm in

00:58:43.230 --> 00:58:49.410
the ice cream or the rice category, and the
answer is yes, but so far it only really works

00:58:49.410 --> 00:58:53.280
in Israel, if you're eating the Israeli diet,
but then the more interesting question is,

00:58:53.280 --> 00:58:56.750
you know, suppose I’m in the category of
people who should eat rice and I want to be

00:58:56.750 --> 00:59:01.760
in the category of people who should eat ice
cream, can I change my microbiome to get there?

00:59:01.760 --> 00:59:05.550
And that's the kind of thing we don't know
yet, but it's plausible based on what we can

00:59:05.550 --> 00:59:06.600
do in mice.

00:59:06.600 --> 00:59:14.310
Will we one day be able to match the things
that we need to be doing to our microbiome,

00:59:14.310 --> 00:59:17.180
sort of the way we might other things?

00:59:17.180 --> 00:59:21.859
In other words, use information about our
microbiome to inform what what medicines might

00:59:21.859 --> 00:59:24.410
work best for us?

00:59:24.410 --> 00:59:28.240
That that's very plausible and again, in the
context of research studies, figuring out

00:59:28.240 --> 00:59:32.470
if a particular drug, whether it's a painkiller
or whether it's a heart drug, whether it's

00:59:32.470 --> 00:59:37.720
a checkpoint inhibitor for cancer, we'll work
on that based on the microbiome.

00:59:37.720 --> 00:59:39.710
That works really nicely at a research level.

00:59:39.710 --> 00:59:44.900
It's not yet ready for clinical use because
it has to get through FDA approval and so

00:59:44.900 --> 00:59:47.580
on, but that's probably coming pretty soon.

00:59:47.580 --> 00:59:53.260
That's a huge frontier because with every
medicine that we use, we know that there's

00:59:53.260 --> 00:59:56.680
tremendous variation in how well it works.

00:59:56.680 --> 00:59:59.450
What's the level of toxicity?

00:59:59.450 --> 01:00:03.680
And there's more and more evidence that your
microbiome is one of the controlling factors.

01:00:03.680 --> 01:00:09.310
So if we could understand that, we could make
drugs work better and less toxic, more effective.

01:00:09.310 --> 01:00:12.010
Or choose the right drug for the right person.

01:00:12.010 --> 01:00:17.180
Billions of dollars have been spent looking
for those answers in the human genome, but

01:00:17.180 --> 01:00:20.840
there are probably not nearly as much there
as there are in the microbiome.

01:00:20.840 --> 01:00:23.920
And the other thing is that you can't change
your human genome right?

01:00:23.920 --> 01:00:28.300
To change it from being a non responder to
a responder, but you may be able to change

01:00:28.300 --> 01:00:29.300
your microbiome.

01:00:29.300 --> 01:00:36.650
In just the same way that, uh, that variable
response to drugs is so individualized and

01:00:36.650 --> 01:00:40.710
potentially related to the microbiome, so
is the individual response to environmental

01:00:40.710 --> 01:00:43.599
chemicals, heavy metals in the environment.

01:00:43.599 --> 01:00:45.820
Look at lead, look at arsenic.

01:00:45.820 --> 01:00:52.650
There are huge populations at risk and yet
within a population of identical exposure,

01:00:52.650 --> 01:00:54.640
there's a lot of variability in outcome.

01:00:54.640 --> 01:01:00.140
And that's where I think we could have a huge
impact in public health on both predicting

01:01:00.140 --> 01:01:06.119
who is more or less likely to suffer the ill
effects of let's say lead because there are

01:01:06.119 --> 01:01:09.280
genes in our gut microbiome that manage these
metals.

01:01:09.280 --> 01:01:11.780
They've seen them for eons.

01:01:11.780 --> 01:01:15.300
And if we can predict that, that would be
great and if we could do something about it,

01:01:15.300 --> 01:01:16.680
it'd be even better.

01:01:16.680 --> 01:01:21.230
You mentioned response to different medications
and there is some work in antidepressants.

01:01:21.230 --> 01:01:22.230
Is that correct?

01:01:22.230 --> 01:01:23.609
That there's-

01:01:23.609 --> 01:01:28.960
My lab is actually starting that work with
some collaborators at Wisconsin, so we don't

01:01:28.960 --> 01:01:30.200
have anything to tell you yet.

01:01:30.200 --> 01:01:31.840
What's the goal there though?

01:01:31.840 --> 01:01:32.840
The goal is-

01:01:32.840 --> 01:01:36.420
because we all know that it's, for people
who are depressed, it might take several trials

01:01:36.420 --> 01:01:38.099
to find a drug that works for them.

01:01:38.099 --> 01:01:40.210
There's no one pill that works for everybody
all the time.

01:01:40.210 --> 01:01:41.210
No.

01:01:41.210 --> 01:01:45.680
And in fact, the efficacy of antidepressants
for any given antidepressant working in any

01:01:45.680 --> 01:01:48.390
given person is the lowest of any drug group.

01:01:48.390 --> 01:01:51.190
So it's about a 20 percent response rate.

01:01:51.190 --> 01:01:55.500
So typically people have to go through four
or five drugs to find one that works, and

01:01:55.500 --> 01:02:00.530
that's a very long and painful and depressing
a process.

01:02:00.530 --> 01:02:06.230
And so if we could upfront decide just by
a microbiome sample, which person is likely

01:02:06.230 --> 01:02:12.970
to respond to which drug, it's a very quick,
uh, and I think short term use of microbiome

01:02:12.970 --> 01:02:19.280
data because we don't even have to understand
the mechanism if, we hope to, but if we find

01:02:19.280 --> 01:02:24.530
such a connection, it would just be a predictor
that could save physicians and patients a

01:02:24.530 --> 01:02:25.530
lot of time.

01:02:25.530 --> 01:02:26.530
A lot of time.

01:02:26.530 --> 01:02:31.151
We already know, for example, that tylenol
response is microbiome driven, so there are

01:02:31.151 --> 01:02:32.960
differences in the human population.

01:02:32.960 --> 01:02:37.089
That's been known for years and there are
several other drugs coming along that look

01:02:37.089 --> 01:02:43.740
like their efficacy is driven or in some cases
toxicity is driven by the microbiome.

01:02:43.740 --> 01:02:49.250
Likewise, anticancer drugs, the big recent
wave and the use of checkpoint inhibitors

01:02:49.250 --> 01:02:55.460
now is clearly related to the presence of
certain kinds of microbes in your gut that

01:02:55.460 --> 01:02:58.020
either make them more or less effective.

01:02:58.020 --> 01:02:59.100
It's incredible.

01:02:59.100 --> 01:03:00.960
We must discuss probiotics.

01:03:00.960 --> 01:03:05.550
We've only got three minutes, 40 seconds left,
yes or no.

01:03:05.550 --> 01:03:06.550
Marty.

01:03:06.550 --> 01:03:08.820
The future is great for probiotics.

01:03:08.820 --> 01:03:13.750
We're all thinking that there will be probiotics
that people can use.

01:03:13.750 --> 01:03:22.150
Most of the materials out there for sale in
the health food store, grocery store pharmacy,

01:03:22.150 --> 01:03:25.920
very variable, almost completely untested.

01:03:25.920 --> 01:03:28.960
So caution on the probiotic thing.

01:03:28.960 --> 01:03:30.320
It's, it's hot right now.

01:03:30.320 --> 01:03:31.320
It’s really hot.

01:03:31.320 --> 01:03:33.670
It's really millions of people take it every
day.

01:03:33.670 --> 01:03:34.670
Yep.

01:03:34.670 --> 01:03:36.730
Astonishingly little data.

01:03:36.730 --> 01:03:37.730
Astonishing little data.

01:03:37.730 --> 01:03:38.730
Okay.

01:03:38.730 --> 01:03:43.470
But there are some really good studies that
show effects of, of probiotics.

01:03:43.470 --> 01:03:48.859
Some of the lactobacillus in particular, lactobacillus
reuteri, which seems to be throughout the

01:03:48.859 --> 01:03:54.070
Mammalian world, which suggests that maybe
there's some coevolution of mammals with that,

01:03:54.070 --> 01:03:58.950
that bug that seems to have a reproducible
effects in people.

01:03:58.950 --> 01:04:00.089
There is even-

01:04:00.089 --> 01:04:02.750
What do you mean by reproducible effects?

01:04:02.750 --> 01:04:09.730
Well, in one case it had effects on the immune
system and um, it, it reduced inflammation.

01:04:09.730 --> 01:04:13.760
And then there's another study that shows
a lactobacillus that reduces depression.

01:04:13.760 --> 01:04:17.000
That was this one study from England that
hasn't been reproduced.

01:04:17.000 --> 01:04:21.670
So I think there are several things coming
along that we will have data, but we just

01:04:21.670 --> 01:04:22.670
don't have enough now.

01:04:22.670 --> 01:04:24.010
But save your money for the moment.

01:04:24.010 --> 01:04:25.010
What about you Rob?

01:04:25.010 --> 01:04:26.010
Yes.

01:04:26.010 --> 01:04:28.800
So just like with drugs, there are, there
are studies that particular probiotics work

01:04:28.800 --> 01:04:32.560
for particular clinical indications, but that
doesn't mean that you should just take a whole

01:04:32.560 --> 01:04:36.900
lot of probiotics all the time, even if you're
not sick any more than you should take a whole

01:04:36.900 --> 01:04:39.770
lot of drugs all the time, even when you're
not sick.

01:04:39.770 --> 01:04:40.770
Okay.

01:04:40.770 --> 01:04:44.430
There's enough to say that the future is bright.

01:04:44.430 --> 01:04:48.230
The present is a little bit murky.

01:04:48.230 --> 01:04:52.060
David Mills at UC Davis did a study and showed
that about half of the things that he could

01:04:52.060 --> 01:04:58.040
buy in a health food store when sequenced
in his lab, were not even the organism that

01:04:58.040 --> 01:05:04.290
the label said they were, and we know that
this, not just the species, but the strain

01:05:04.290 --> 01:05:05.290
matters.

01:05:05.290 --> 01:05:10.400
So I think we're just working with really
imperfect material and we can't know yet.

01:05:10.400 --> 01:05:14.190
The probiotic manufacturing industry is the
wild west.

01:05:14.190 --> 01:05:15.400
The wild west.

01:05:15.400 --> 01:05:16.400
Okay.

01:05:16.400 --> 01:05:17.400
Caveat emptor.

01:05:17.400 --> 01:05:18.570
Buyer beware.

01:05:18.570 --> 01:05:19.570
Okay.

01:05:19.570 --> 01:05:21.570
Marty, I just wanted to end.

01:05:21.570 --> 01:05:27.200
You have a particular vision for what the
future might look like when we've understood

01:05:27.200 --> 01:05:29.930
the role of the microbiome in our own health.

01:05:29.930 --> 01:05:35.150
You say that particularly pediatricians are
gonna have a very different way to examine

01:05:35.150 --> 01:05:36.150
patients.

01:05:36.150 --> 01:05:37.150
What's that gonna look like?

01:05:37.150 --> 01:05:38.150
And.

01:05:38.150 --> 01:05:40.490
Well, certainly we're going to live happily
ever after.

01:05:40.490 --> 01:05:41.490
Okay.

01:05:41.490 --> 01:05:47.079
But beyond that, I, I think, uh, I, I envisioned
that the doctor of the future isn't gonna

01:05:47.079 --> 01:05:50.940
examine babies and they're gonna examine babies
diapers, and they're going to analyze those

01:05:50.940 --> 01:05:56.970
debt, those diapers with the knowledge that
is being created in laboratories all over

01:05:56.970 --> 01:06:01.210
the world, and try to use that information
to optimize health.

01:06:01.210 --> 01:06:06.609
And if there are particular microbes that
are missing in that baby that every baby should

01:06:06.609 --> 01:06:12.320
have, I believe that the doctors of the future
will be giving those microbes back and that

01:06:12.320 --> 01:06:17.870
there may be specific microbes that, that
baby is missing that they need, that will

01:06:17.870 --> 01:06:20.840
become part of the medical care of the future.

01:06:20.840 --> 01:06:23.390
Not Tomorrow, maybe 20 years from now.

01:06:23.390 --> 01:06:24.500
I'm not sure.

01:06:24.500 --> 01:06:25.500
Stay tuned.

01:06:25.500 --> 01:06:26.500
Right.

01:06:26.500 --> 01:06:27.500
Well, we'll get back to you on that.

01:06:27.500 --> 01:06:28.500
Right.

01:06:28.500 --> 01:06:30.570
I want to thank the panel here today, David.

01:06:30.570 --> 01:06:31.800
Rob, Jo, Marty.

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:33.700
Thank you so much for this incredible discussion.

